Usages:
  job_record_subclassing: |
    A new kind of durable background job is added by subclassing `JobRecord`, adding whatever
    extra columns that job type needs, and overriding the type's stable record-type identifier.
    The base class owns only the fields every job shares: identity, failure count, and status.

Annotations: |
  This cell defines the base persisted unit of Signal's durable background-job framework —
  the row that survives an app relaunch so a queued job is not lost. It intentionally knows
  nothing about how a job is executed; execution is the responsibility of the runner
  framework documented separately (see the sibling Jobs cell, which imports this type).
  Use `job_record_subclassing` when describing how a new job type is introduced.

---

"JobRecord()":
  location: JobRecord.swift
  annotations: |
    Base class for every durable, persisted background job. Concrete job types subclass this
    and add their own payload columns.

    Use `job_record_subclassing` for the extension pattern new job types follow.
  properties:
    "uniqueId -> String": |
      Stable identifier for this job record row.
    "failureCount -> Int": |
      Number of times this job has been attempted and failed so far.
  methods:
    "addFailure(tx: DBWriteTransaction)": |
      Increments the failure count and persists the change; called by the runner after a
      retryable failure.

